<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-pointwise-and-uniform-convergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Pointwise and uniform convergence
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine whether the following sequences of real-valued functions converge or diverge both pointwise and uniformly in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If the given sequence converges pointwise, find the pointwise limit. Express your answer using limit notation. If the given sequence converges uniformly, find the uniform limit.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> of real-valued functions <m> a_{ n } \colon \R \to \R </m> defined by the formulae
        
        <md>
          a_{ n } \of{ x } = \of{ x - \frac{ 1 }{ n } }^{ 2 }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges pointwise in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges pointwise, find the pointwise limit. Express your answer using limit notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> a \colon \R \to \R </m> be the function defined by the formula <m> a \of{ x } = x^{ 2 } </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> a </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ a_{ n } } = a
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m> a \colon \R \to \R </m> be the function defined by the formula <m> a \of{ x } = x^{ 2 } </m>. Fix a point <m> x \in \R </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. We observe that if <m> n \gt \frac{ 4 \abs{ x } }{ \epsilon } </m>, then
          
          <md>
            \frac{ 2 \abs{ x } }{ n } \lt \frac{ 2 \abs{ x } }{ \of{ \frac{ 4 \abs{ x } }{ \epsilon } } } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 }
          </md>.
          
          Similarly, we observe that if <m> n \gt \sqrt{ \frac{ 2 }{ \epsilon } } </m>, then
          
          <md>
            \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } \lt \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ \sqrt{ \frac{ 2 }{ \epsilon } } }^{ 2 } } = \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 }
          </md>.
          
          The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \max \of{ \set{ \frac{ 4 \abs{ x } }{ \epsilon } , \sqrt{ \frac{ 2 }{ \epsilon } } } }
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ a_{ n } \of{ x } , a \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \of{ x - \frac{ 1 }{ n } }^{ 2 } - x^{ 2 } } = \abs{ x^{ 2 } - \of{ \frac{ 2 }{ n } } x + \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } - x^{ 2 } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \abs{ -\frac{ 2 }{ n } x + \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } } \leq \frac{ 2 \abs{ x } }{ n } + \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 2 } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } \of{ x } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> a \of{ x } </m>. Since this holds for all points <m> x \in \R </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> a </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ a_{ n } } = a
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges uniformly in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges uniformly, find the uniform limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          The sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          The dependence of <m> n_{ \epsilon } </m> on <m> x </m> in the above solution for (a) is a good sign that the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly. Indeed, we observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ a_{ n } \of{ n } , a \of{ n } } \amp = \abs{ \of{ n - \frac{ 1 }{ n } }^{ 2 } - n^{ 2 } } = \abs{ n^{ 2 } - \of{ \frac{ 2 }{ n } } n + \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } - n^{ 2 } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \abs{ -2 + \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ 2 } } } \geq 2 - \frac{ 1 }{ n } \geq 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all positive integers <m> n \geq 1 </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly to <m> a </m>. Since <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> a </m>, this implies that the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> of real-valued functions <m> b_{ n } \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> defined by the formulae
        
        <md>
          b_{ n } \of{ x } = \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges pointwise in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges pointwise, find the pointwise limit. Express your answer using limit notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> b \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> b \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> b </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ b_{ n } } = b
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m> b \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> b \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Fix a point <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \epsilon }
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ b_{ n } \of{ x } , b \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ n } \leq \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ n_{ \epsilon } } \lt \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \epsilon } } } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } \of{ x } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> b \of{ x } </m>. Since this holds for all points <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> b </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ b_{ n } } = b
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges uniformly in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges uniformly, find the uniform limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> b \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> b \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> b </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ b_{ n } } = b
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          The dependence of <m> n_{ \epsilon } </m> on <m> x </m> in the above solution for (a) is a good sign that the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly. However, the bounds on the input <m> -10 \leq x \leq 10 </m> allow us to obtain uniform convergence.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \frac{ 100 }{ \epsilon }
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ b_{ n } \of{ x } , b \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ n } \leq \frac{ 10^{ 2 } }{ n_{ \epsilon } } \lt \frac{ 100 }{ \of{ \frac{ 100 }{ \epsilon } } } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ b_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> b </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ b_{ n } } = b
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> of real-valued functions <m> c_{ n } \colon \R \to \R </m> defined by the formulae
        
        <md>
          c_{ n } \of{ x } = \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges pointwise in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges pointwise, find the pointwise limit. Express your answer using limit notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> c \colon \R \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> c \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> c </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ c_{ n } } = c
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m> c \colon \R \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> c \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Fix a point <m> x \in \R </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \epsilon }
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ c_{ n } \of{ x } , c \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ x^{ 2 } }{ n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ n } \leq \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ n_{ \epsilon } } \lt \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \of{ \frac{ \abs{ x }^{ 2 } }{ \epsilon } } } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } \of{ x } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> c \of{ x } </m>. Since this holds for all points <m> x \in \R </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> c </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ c_{ n } } = c
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges uniformly in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges uniformly, find the uniform limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          The sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          The dependence of <m> n_{ \epsilon } </m> on <m> x </m> in the above solution for (a) is a good sign that the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly. Indeed, we observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ c_{ n } \of{ \sqrt{ n } } , c \of{ \sqrt{ n } } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ \of{ \sqrt{ n } }^{ 2 } }{ n } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ n }{ n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \abs{ 1 } = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all positive integers <m> n \geq 1 </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly to <m> c </m>. Since <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> c </m>, this implies that the sequence <m> \of{ c_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of real-valued functions <m> d_{ n } \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> defined by the formulae
        
        <md>
          d_{ n } \of{ x } = \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges pointwise in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges pointwise, find the pointwise limit. Express your answer using limit notation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> d \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> d \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> d </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ d_{ n } } = d
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m> d \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> d \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Fix a point <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \log_{ 3 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } - x
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ d_{ n } \of{ x } , d \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp \leq \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n_{ \epsilon } } } \lt \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + \log_{ 3 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } - x } } = \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ \log_{ 3 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } \of{ x } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> d \of{ x } </m>. Since this holds for all points <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>, the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges pointwise to <m> d </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ d_{ n } } = d
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges or diverges uniformly in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on the real numbers <m> \R </m>. If it converges uniformly, find the uniform limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <answer>
        <p>
          Let <m> d \colon \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } \to \R </m> be the constant function defined by the formula <m> d \of{ x } = 0 </m>. Then the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> b </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ d_{ n } } = d
          </md>.
        </p>
      </answer>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          The dependence of <m> n_{ \epsilon } </m> on <m> x </m> in the above solution for (a) is a good sign that the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge uniformly. However, the bounds on the input <m> -10 \leq x \leq 10 </m> allow us to obtain uniform convergence.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number. The Archimedean principle implies that
          
          <md>
            n_{ \epsilon } \gt \ln \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } + 10
          </md>
          
          for some natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m>. We observe that
          
          <md>
            <mrow>
              d \of{ d_{ n } \of{ x } , d \of{ x } } \amp = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ x + n } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp \leq \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ -10 + n_{ \epsilon } } } \lt \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ -10 + \log_{ 3 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } + 10 } } = \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ \log_{ 3 } \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } } }
            </mrow>
            
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } } } = \epsilon
            </mrow>
          </md>
          
          for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> x \in \clint{ -10 }{ 10 } </m>. Thus the sequence <m> \of{ d_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> d </m>; that is,
          
          <md>
            \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ d_{ n } } = d
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
